<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

// Each row is [concept, difficulty, stem, keyed answer, three plausible distractors,
// explanation, supporting source IDs]. The question-writing is original and
// deliberately stays inside the existing source-readiness headroom.
return [
    'abnormal' => [
        ['psych-abnormal-disorders-symptoms','medium','A vignette describes recurring panic episodes, but gives no information about duration, avoidance, or impact on daily activity. Which interpretation is best supported?','The episodes warrant further assessment, while the available details are insufficient for a disorder-level conclusion.',['The episodes establish a panic disorder because they recur.','The episodes are not clinically relevant unless work or school has stopped.','The person should be classified by the most severe possible explanation.'],'Repeated episodes are relevant evidence, but classification depends on the full pattern and applicable criteria. The missing duration, avoidance, context, and functioning details should not be invented.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','who-icd11-cddr-2024','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-abnormal-disorders-symptoms','easy','A person reports low mood on several days after a recent loss but continues usual responsibilities and describes supportive relationships. What should an initial interview do?','Explore the timing, pattern, context, distress, and functioning before drawing a classification conclusion.',['Treat the report as proof of a depressive disorder because low mood is present.','Dismiss the report because responsibilities are still being completed.','Infer that grief is the only possible explanation from the recent loss.'],'A symptom and a life event both matter, but neither settles classification. Gathering context and functional evidence avoids premature labeling and avoids dismissing distress.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','who-icd11-cddr-2024','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-abnormal-disorders-symptoms','medium','Two clients report difficulty concentrating. One describes a recent medication change; the other reports a long-standing pattern across settings. What is the most defensible use of this difference?','Treat it as a reason to gather distinct histories rather than assuming the shared symptom has one cause.',['Use the same classification for both because the reported symptom matches.','Use concentration difficulty alone to select a disorder category.','Ignore the medication history because it is not a psychological measure.'],'The same symptom can have different contexts and possible explanations. Classification requires the relevant history and should not collapse distinct cases into one category.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','who-icd11-cddr-2024','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-abnormal-disorders-symptoms','hard','A clinician records both a client’s unusual belief and the client’s ability to question it when presented with contrary evidence. Why preserve both observations?','They describe different features of the experience and may matter to interpretation, course, and assessment.',['The client’s ability to question the belief proves that the reported experience is fabricated.','The unusual belief alone is sufficient, so the second observation adds no useful information.','The observations should be combined into one severity score without context.'],'Assessment should retain clinically relevant detail rather than reducing a complex presentation to one feature. The observations may inform further evaluation but do not independently establish a diagnosis.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','who-icd11-cddr-2024']],
        ['psych-abnormal-etiology','medium','A family history and a recent stressful event are both present in a case. What conclusion best reflects an interactional account of possible etiology?','Both may be relevant, but the available facts do not show that either one alone caused the presentation.',['The family history determines the outcome regardless of later experience.','The stressful event is sufficient to explain every reported feature.','The two factors cancel each other, so no further history is needed.'],'Biological, psychological, and social influences may interact. Their presence supports careful assessment of pathways and timing, not a single-cause conclusion.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e','who-icd11-cddr-2024']],
        ['psych-abnormal-etiology','easy','A researcher finds that a risk factor is more common in a group with a disorder. What does this group comparison establish by itself?','An association in the studied groups, subject to design and measurement limits.',['That the risk factor caused the disorder in each member of the group.','That removing the factor would prevent every case.','That the observed difference cannot reflect selection or confounding.'],'A group association does not establish individual causation or rule out other explanations. The design, timing, sampling, and measurement determine what further claims are supported.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-abnormal-etiology','medium','A client’s distress increased after a move, while similar concerns were reported before the move. Which assessment question is most useful next?','How did the pattern change over time and across settings before and after the move?',['Was the move the single event that caused the entire history?','Which family member should be held responsible for the change?','Can the earlier concerns be omitted because the current episode is more intense?'],'A timeline helps distinguish onset, exacerbation, and context. It avoids attributing a long-standing pattern to one recent event without evidence.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e','who-icd11-cddr-2024']],
        ['psych-abnormal-etiology','hard','A study reports that sleep disruption and distress vary together over several weeks, but does not manipulate either variable. Which statement is most accurate?','The measures covary over the observed period; direction and other influences remain unresolved.',['Sleep disruption caused the distress because it was measured each week.','Distress caused every change in sleep because symptoms were also recorded.','The variables have no relationship because neither was experimentally assigned.'],'Repeated observation can establish a temporal pattern of association, but without an appropriate design it does not isolate direction or exclude shared influences.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-abnormal-interventions','medium','A service compares clients’ scores before and after a new program, with no comparison group and several other service changes occurring at the same time. What can the report reasonably say?','Scores changed after the program, but the design cannot attribute the change to that program alone.',['The program caused the improvement because the later average is higher.','The program had no effect because other changes also occurred.','The score change proves the program works for future clients.'],'A before-and-after difference is descriptive. Concurrent changes, selection, measurement effects, and time can offer other explanations; stronger comparative evidence is needed for causal claims.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-abnormal-interventions','easy','A practitioner is asked whether a support plan is helping. Which information best addresses that question?','The stated goals, repeated outcome evidence, client context, and possible unintended effects.',['Whether the plan is popular with staff who use it.','Whether one favorable result occurred immediately after it began.','Whether the plan is commonly used in other settings.'],'Evaluation should connect outcomes to the stated purpose and include relevant context over time. Popularity or one observation does not establish benefit or fit.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e','apa-psychological-assessment-guidelines-2020']],
        ['psych-abnormal-interventions','medium','A client reports that a support has not helped, while a score on one measure has improved. What is the most appropriate next step?','Review the measure’s meaning alongside the client’s goals, experience, and other relevant evidence.',['Continue unchanged because the score is the most objective information.','Stop the support immediately because the client’s report disproves the score.','Report success using the score and leave the disagreement out of the record.'],'Different evidence sources may answer different questions. A careful review explains the score’s limits and the client’s experience before deciding whether to adjust the plan.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','apa-psychological-assessment-guidelines-2020','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-abnormal-interventions','hard','A service reports fewer missed appointments after changing its reminder process and extending clinic hours in the same month. Which evaluation step would improve the causal interpretation?','Collect a suitable comparison or phased evidence and document the timing of both changes.',['Attribute the reduction to the reminder process because it was introduced first.','Attribute the reduction to longer hours because access is a likely concern.','Average the counts from both months and describe the result as causal.'],'When interventions change together, an appropriate comparison or staged design can help separate their contributions. The current observation supports a change over time, not a unique cause.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-abnormal-sociocultural-ethics','medium','A staff member asks a psychometrician to recommend a specific treatment after reviewing a screening report. What response best respects professional scope?','Explain what the screening evidence can and cannot show, then refer the treatment decision to an appropriately qualified professional.',['Recommend a treatment because screening results identify the most effective option.','Choose a treatment that has worked for other people with similar scores.','Decline to explain the report because treatment is outside the role.'],'A psychometrician can communicate assessment limits and appropriate next steps without making a treatment recommendation beyond competence. Referral should preserve useful explanation and continuity.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','apa-ethics-code-2017','apa-psychological-assessment-guidelines-2020']],
        ['psych-abnormal-manifestations','medium','A client describes hearing a voice only while falling asleep, with no similar experience during the day. Which next step is most appropriate?','Clarify the timing, sleep context, frequency, distress, and associated functioning before interpreting it.',['Classify the report as a psychotic symptom because it involves a voice.','Dismiss the experience because it occurs near sleep.','Infer a sleep disorder without asking about any other symptoms.'],'The timing and context may affect interpretation, but neither establishes nor rules out a condition. A fuller description is needed before selecting a framework.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','who-icd11-cddr-2024','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-abnormal-manifestations','easy','A case describes repeated checking that takes time but does not say whether the person feels compelled or distressed. What detail would most improve interpretation?','The person’s experience of the checking, its duration, context, and effect on daily functioning.',['The number of objects in the room where the checking occurs.','Whether a friend has noticed the behavior once.','Whether the behavior seems unusual to the interviewer.'],'Observable repetition alone does not explain its meaning or impact. Relevant subjective experience, context, time, and functioning help determine what additional assessment is needed.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','who-icd11-cddr-2024']],
        ['psych-abnormal-manifestations','medium','A person reports intense worry before public presentations but not in ordinary conversations or other settings. How should this pattern be recorded?','As a context-specific report, with further questions about persistence, avoidance, distress, and impact.',['As evidence that the person has a pervasive anxiety pattern across settings.','As proof that the person has no anxiety concern because it is limited to one setting.','As a stable trait without asking how long the pattern has occurred.'],'The stated context narrows what is known but does not settle duration, significance, or classification. Recording the pattern accurately guides the next assessment questions.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','who-icd11-cddr-2024','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-abnormal-manifestations','hard','A client’s account differs from a relative’s account about when a behavior began. What should an assessor do with the discrepancy?','Record both accounts, clarify their observation contexts, and seek further information where appropriate.',['Choose the relative’s account because family members are more objective.','Choose the client’s account because the client is the only valid source.','Average the reported dates to create one onset estimate.'],'Disagreement can reflect different access, memory, or interpretation. Preserving who observed what and when is more defensible than selecting or averaging accounts without evidence.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','apa-psychological-assessment-guidelines-2020']],
        ['psych-abnormal-sociocultural-ethics','medium','A behavior is described as unusual by a practitioner who is unfamiliar with the client’s cultural setting. What is the most responsible response?','Ask how the behavior is understood in context and compare it with appropriate, culturally informed evidence.',['Use the practitioner’s expectation as the standard because professional judgment is neutral.','Treat the behavior as typical in that culture without asking the client.','Remove the observation from consideration because culture affects all behavior.'],'Cultural context can inform meaning without deciding it in advance. The assessor should avoid both ethnocentric judgment and unsupported assumptions about a group.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','apa-psychological-assessment-guidelines-2020','apa-ethics-code-2017']],
        ['psych-abnormal-sociocultural-ethics','easy','A colleague asks for a client’s assessment results in a shared staff chat “so the team can help.” What must be checked first?','Whether the colleague is authorized and needs the information for a defined purpose, and what minimum disclosure is appropriate.',['Whether the chat is convenient for most team members.','Whether the colleague already knows the client socially.','Whether the results can be shortened before they are posted.'],'Convenience or familiarity does not establish authorization. Confidential information should be shared only through an appropriate channel, for a justified purpose, and to the necessary extent.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','apa-ethics-code-2017','apa-psychological-assessment-guidelines-2020']],
        ['psych-abnormal-sociocultural-ethics','medium','A translated test has not been studied with the community in which a school plans to use it. What should the psychometrician advise?','Review language, cultural relevance, and evidence for the intended population before interpreting scores.',['Use the existing norms because a translated item has the same meaning.','Translate the score cutoff but keep the interpretation unchanged.','Avoid all assessment with members of the community.'],'Translation alone does not establish comparable meaning or score interpretation. Review should be evidence based and proportionate; lack of one measure does not justify abandoning all assessment.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','apa-psychological-assessment-guidelines-2020']],
        ['psych-abnormal-sociocultural-ethics','hard','A supervisor asks for a diagnostic label to be placed in a public-facing school report, although the report’s purpose is to describe learning supports. What is the best response?','Clarify the report purpose, limit disclosure to relevant authorized information, and discuss a less identifying description of support needs.',['Include the label because supervisors are always entitled to all assessment data.','Remove every mention of support needs to avoid any privacy concern.','Use the label but omit the assessment context so the report stays brief.'],'A report should be purpose limited and respectful. Relevant support information can often be conveyed without unnecessary diagnostic disclosure; authorization and applicable rules must still be checked.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','apa-ethics-code-2017','apa-psychological-assessment-guidelines-2020']],
    ],
    'industrial' => [
        ['psych-industrial-change-development','medium','A manager wants to attribute a productivity increase to a new coaching program, but the team also received new equipment at the same time. What conclusion is justified now?','Productivity increased after both changes; the available comparison does not isolate coaching as the cause.',['Coaching caused the increase because it addressed worker behavior.','The equipment caused the increase because it was a tangible change.','Neither change could have contributed because they began together.'],'Concurrent changes prevent a simple causal attribution. The report should describe the timing and seek a better comparison before assigning effects to one intervention.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-industrial-change-development','easy','A company plans to introduce a new shift schedule. Which preparation best supports a fair evaluation of its effects?','Define relevant outcomes and affected groups before rollout, then collect comparable baseline and follow-up evidence.',['Ask managers afterward whether the schedule felt more efficient.','Compare the first week’s output with the highest-output week last year.','Use attendance alone because it is easy to count.'],'Predefined outcomes and comparable evidence reduce cherry-picking. A fair evaluation should consider how the schedule affects performance and employees, not only a convenient metric.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e','apa-ethics-code-2017']],
        ['psych-industrial-change-development','hard','An organization reports that a training group has higher sales than a nontraining group, but the groups were formed by managers based on prior performance. What is the main inference limit?','The groups may have differed before training, so the sales gap cannot be attributed to training alone.',['Sales is not a relevant outcome for any training evaluation.','Manager assignment ensures that the groups are equivalent.','A higher sales average proves the training is effective for every employee.'],'Nonrandom selection can create preexisting group differences. Baseline information or a stronger design is needed to estimate the training contribution.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-industrial-hr-development-management','medium','A role description lists “good attitude” as a required qualification. What is the best job-analysis follow-up?','Identify the work behaviors and conditions the phrase is meant to represent, then define job-relevant evidence.',['Keep the phrase because supervisors can recognize attitude when they see it.','Replace it with a personality test score without checking the role.','Remove every behavioral requirement because attitudes cannot be observed.'],'Vague language can invite inconsistent or biased decisions. Job analysis should connect requirements to observable work demands and appropriate assessment evidence.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','apa-personnel-selection-principles-2018']],
        ['psych-industrial-hr-development-management','easy','An applicant requests a screen reader for a selection task. What should the organization do before administering the assessment?','Review the task’s essential construct, the requested access support, and the applicable accommodation process.',['Deny the request because all applicants must use identical tools.','Grant the support without checking whether it changes the task being measured.','Replace the assessment with an interview for every applicant.'],'The decision should preserve the intended measurement while providing appropriate access under applicable rules. Identical conditions are not automatically equivalent or fair.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','apa-personnel-selection-principles-2018','apa-ethics-code-2017']],
        ['psych-industrial-hr-development-management','medium','A supervisor wants to select a candidate using a test that predicts a different role’s performance. What evidence should be reviewed?','Whether the assessment is job relevant and supported for this use, population, and decision context.',['Whether the test is popular with other departments.','Whether the candidate’s score is higher than the current employees’ average.','Whether the test manual contains a general claim of reliability.'],'A measure’s reputation or reliability alone does not establish validity for a particular selection use. The intended job, population, criterion, and consequences matter.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','apa-personnel-selection-principles-2018']],
        ['psych-industrial-organization-theory','medium','Two departments report different morale after a restructuring, but one had a much heavier workload during the survey period. What should the analyst do?','Interpret the results with the workload context and other relevant organizational conditions.',['Treat morale as a fixed feature of each department.','Average the scores and omit department differences.','Attribute the difference to the restructuring because it preceded the survey.'],'Organizational measures are shaped by context and exposure. A group difference needs interpretation that considers workload and other conditions rather than a single structural explanation.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-industrial-organization-theory','easy','A manager proposes changing reporting lines because several employees say they are unsure who approves routine work. What information should be gathered first?','Map actual decision routes, task dependencies, and where responsibility becomes unclear.',['Move every approval to the manager to eliminate uncertainty.','Ask only senior staff which reporting chart they prefer.','Wait for a formal complaint because informal confusion is not evidence.'],'A structural change should address the work process that creates the problem. Mapping actual decisions and dependencies can distinguish a reporting issue from unclear procedures or resources.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-industrial-organization-theory','hard','A company compares turnover across units but defines turnover differently in each unit. What is the first analytic problem to resolve?','Use consistent operational definitions and comparable time periods before interpreting differences.',['Rank the units by their reported percentage and adjust later.','Treat each unit’s definition as equally comparable because both use percentages.','Combine all records and let the largest unit determine the result.'],'A common metric requires consistent definitions, denominators, and time windows. Otherwise, apparent differences may reflect measurement choices rather than organizational outcomes.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-industrial-structures-systems','medium','A factory’s safety reports show repeated delays at a handoff between maintenance and production. What should the analyst examine?','The handoff responsibilities, information flow, timing, and resources across both units.',['Which unit has the more cooperative employees.','Whether production can accept all maintenance decisions without review.','Whether the delay can be removed from the report to protect morale.'],'A systems view inspects dependencies and information at the boundary. Assigning blame or deleting the signal misses how the work process produces the delay.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e','apa-ethics-code-2017']],
        ['psych-industrial-structures-systems','easy','A staffing model uses the annual average workload, although demand peaks sharply during two months. What limitation should be stated?','The annual average may conceal peak-period staffing needs and service delays.',['The model is invalid because averages cannot be used in staffing.','Peak months should be ignored because they occur only twice.','The average guarantees enough staff whenever demand rises.'],'An average summarizes the year but can hide important variation. Planning should examine the distribution and periods that affect service quality and employee workload.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-industrial-structures-systems','hard','A team proposes replacing a manual review step with an automated decision rule. What should be assessed before implementation?','Error patterns, affected groups, exception handling, oversight, and responsibility for review.',['Whether the automated rule makes decisions faster than the manual process.','Whether the rule reproduces the current decision pattern exactly.','Whether a vendor states that the tool is suitable for organizations.'],'Efficiency and similarity to past decisions are not enough. The system should be evaluated for errors, impact, exceptions, oversight, and accountable use.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','apa-ethics-code-2017','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-industrial-team-dynamics','medium','In a meeting, one specialist speaks for most of the time while others hold information about implementation risks. What is a useful facilitation step?','Invite structured input from each relevant role before the team settles on an option.',['Let the most senior member summarize the risks from experience.','End discussion early so the team can maintain a positive relationship.','Ask quieter members to send concerns only after the decision is final.'],'Structured participation can surface distributed information before commitment. It does not guarantee agreement, but it improves the evidence available to the decision.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-industrial-team-dynamics','easy','Two coworkers disagree about whether a missed deadline resulted from poor planning or an unexpected dependency. What should their manager ask first?','Reconstruct the task sequence, commitments, and dependency information available at the time.',['Which coworker usually makes more reliable estimates.','Who should receive the formal warning to prevent future conflict.','Whether the team can agree on one explanation before reviewing records.'],'A timeline and shared evidence can distinguish a planning issue from a dependency change. Personal reputation or premature blame does not establish what happened.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e']],
        ['psych-industrial-team-dynamics','hard','A team’s output rose after it adopted peer monitoring, but members also worked overtime during the same period. Which report is most responsible?','Describe the observed change and both concurrent conditions, and avoid claiming the monitoring caused the increase.',['Credit peer monitoring because the team chose the practice.','Credit overtime because it directly increased hours available.','Conclude that peer monitoring is effective whenever output rises.'],'The data show an outcome change under multiple simultaneous conditions. A cautious report preserves the observation and the alternative explanations for later evaluation.',['prc-psychometrician-enhanced-tos-2023','openstax-psychology-2e','apa-ethics-code-2017']],
    ],
];
